+# Decision: Change delivery route
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision; a delivery route carries those committed revisions to connected browsers. The route must serve up to 800 simultaneous browsers per event, show a committed change within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, let a browser recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes disconnected, and retain seven days of revisions.
+
+The choice is load-bearing because it sizes three design nodes at once — the change store (how revisions are indexed and published), delivery (what a delivery session is), and browser sync (how a browser subscribes, reconnects, and catches up). Changing the route later means reworking all three.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option: all three routes can meet the 15-second freshness goal, and the project permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure. The choice is settled by weighted criteria.
+
+- **Vendor coupling and recurring cost — major.** Whether low-latency delivery requires an ongoing dependence on, and recurring payment to, a third-party realtime provider. The project weighs this heavily: it prefers to avoid a recurring vendor bill and the coupling it brings, even at the cost of owning more operations.
+- **Operational burden — major.** How much connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect behavior the project must own and operate. Owning more of this is accepted only when it buys something the project values.
+- **Freshness — moderate.** How quickly a committed revision reaches a connected browser. Subsecond is useful but not required; the goal is 15 seconds.
+- **Change-store read load — moderate.** Whether delivery repeatedly reads stored revisions (polling) or is handed them on commit.
+- **Recovery after disconnection — major.** How a browser that missed revisions recovers the latest board across a gap of up to 20 minutes. Derived from the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board.*
+- **Moving parts — minor.** The number of distinct mechanisms the team must build and maintain.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers request the board — or the changes since a cursor — every five seconds over ordinary HTTPS. The server answers from stored revisions. Delivery is stateless request/response with no persistent connection; a browser's cursor is the last revision it has seen.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — no third party involved.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless, with no connection capacity to manage.
+- Freshness: **partial** — a change lags by up to the poll interval (5 seconds), still within the 15-second goal.
+- Change-store read load: **weak** — every connected browser re-reads on each tick, repeating reads of the same revisions.
+- Recovery: **strong** — a browser simply requests from its last cursor.
+- Moving parts: **strong** — the fewest mechanisms.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest stateless operation, at the cost of repeated reads and a few seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server holds a persistent socket per connected browser and pushes each committed revision as it is committed. Delivery owns connection capacity and session lifecycle; the change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes them reliably; a browser reconnects and catches up from its last revision after a drop.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — no third party involved.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, reconnect, and server-restart handling.
+- Freshness: **strong** — revisions arrive on commit.
+- Change-store read load: **strong** — one publish per revision, not a read per browser per tick.
+- Recovery: **partial** — requires reconnect plus catch-up against retained revisions.
+- Moving parts: **weak** — the most mechanisms to build (sessions, publication, replay).
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates without a realtime vendor, at the cost of owning connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider holds the connections and relays revisions to subscribed browsers. Delivery wraps the provider and reconciles publication; the change store retains revisions for replay; browsers use the provider's subscription and reconnection model.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor coupling: **weak** — ongoing provider dependence and recurring fees.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — the provider owns connection capacity and reconnection.
+- Freshness: **strong** — the provider pushes on publication.
+- Change-store read load: **strong** — publish once to the provider.
+- Recovery: **partial** — recovery depends on the provider's replay and history semantics.
+- Moving parts: **partial** — less connection code, but a provider integration to build and reconcile.
+
+**Summary.** Quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection ops, at the cost of recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets** is chosen. It and the hosted service both deliver on-commit freshness, but the project weighs *vendor coupling and recurring cost* as major and prefers to own operational burden rather than pay a recurring vendor fee or tie the board to a provider. Against polling, it avoids repeating a read per browser per tick and the few seconds of lag. The cost is the largest operational surface: connection capacity, session lifecycle, reconnect, and catch-up. That cost is accepted knowingly, in exchange for low-latency updates with no realtime vendor.
+
+The decision turns on a lean the tenets do not yet state — *own the operational burden rather than couple to a realtime vendor, when it buys a real capability gap*. Recording that lean under `## Tenets` would let future infrastructure choices turn on it.
+
+Committing this route:
+
+- Forecloses the stateless, connection-free operation polling would allow, and the vendor-managed connection capacity a hosted service would provide.
+- Obligates the change store to retain replayable revisions and publish them reliably; delivery to own socket sessions and connection capacity; browser sync to subscribe and to reconnect and catch up.
+- Turns on the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* — a dropped socket must surface as a connectivity state, never as an unchanged board.
